public class AccountOnboardingService {

    /**
     * Creates Accounts and their associated Contacts in a single unit-of-work transaction.
     *
     * @param contactLastNamesByAccountName  Map of Account Name → list of Contact Last Names.
     * @return List of newly created Accounts with their Ids populated.
     */
    public static List<Account> createAccountsWithContacts(
        Map<String, List<String>> contactLastNamesByAccountName
    ) {
        if (contactLastNamesByAccountName == null || contactLastNamesByAccountName.isEmpty()) {
            return new List<Account>();
        }

        fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork uow = new fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork();
        List<Account> newAccounts = new List<Account>();

        for (String accountName : contactLastNamesByAccountName.keySet()) {
            Account acc = new Account(Name = accountName);
            uow.addSObject(acc);
            newAccounts.add(acc);

            List<String> lastNames = contactLastNamesByAccountName.get(accountName);
            if (lastNames != null) {
                for (String lastName : lastNames) {
                    Contact con = new Contact(
                        LastName  = lastName,
                        AccountId = acc.Id
                    );
                    uow.addSObject(con);
                }
            }
        }

        uow.commitWork();

        return newAccounts;
    }
}
```

**How it works**

- **No DML in the service.** Every record is staged via `fflib_SObjectUnitOfWork.addSObject()` and persisted in one atomic call to `commitWork()`. The framework handles the actual `insert` statements internally.
- **Parent/child ordering.** When the Contact's `AccountId` is set to `acc.Id` (which is `null` before insert), the fflib SObjectManager detects the lookup relationship to a parent SObject that is also in the unit of work. On commit it inserts the Account first, back-fills the generated Id onto the child Contact, then inserts the Contact—so the foreign key is always correct.
- **Null / empty guard.** A `null` or empty map short-circuits immediately, returning an empty list with zero DML.
- **Bulk-safety.** The method simply iterates the map and appends to in-memory collections; the fflib framework batches the DML internally, so hundreds (or thousands) of contacts stay well within governor limits in a single transaction.

