public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {

    /**
     * For each lead (same order and size), return the Id of the first Contact
     * whose Email matches the lead's Email (case-insensitive, trimmed), or null
     * if there is no match or the email is blank.
     *
     * No SOQL / DML is performed; all matching is in-memory.
     *
     * Complexity: O(n + m) where n = leads.size, m = contacts.size.
     */
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> results = new List<Id>(leads.size());

        // Build a normalized-email → first-contact-Id map.
        // Because we only insert when the key is absent, the first contact
        // in list order wins when multiple contacts share an email.
        Map<String, Id> contactByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            String key = normalize(c.Email);
            if (key != null && !contactByEmail.containsKey(key)) {
                contactByEmail.put(key, c.Id);
            }
        }

        // One map lookup per lead.
        for (Lead l : leads) {
            String key = normalize(l.Email);
            results.add(key != null ? contactByEmail.get(key) : null);
        }

        return results;
    }

    /**
     * Returns a canonical (trimmed, lower-case) form of the email,
     * or null when the value is null, empty, or whitespace-only.
     */
    private static String normalize(String email) {
        if (email == null) {
            return null;
        }
        String trimmed = email.trim();
        if (trimmed.isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }
        return trimmed.toLowerCase();
    }
}
```

**Why I'd do it differently (Map instead of nested loop):**

The nested loop you sketched is perfectly correct and will fit within the 10-second synchronous CPU limit even at 3 000 × 3 000 (≈ 9 M simple `equals` calls). But the map-based version above is:

| Aspect | Nested loop | Map lookup |
|---|---|---|
| Big-O | O(n × m) ≈ 9 M comparisons | O(n + m) ≈ 6 K comparisons |
| Scales to 10 K × 10 K | 100 M comparisons (riskier) | 20 K comparisons |
| Readability | Slightly more "obvious" | Equally obvious once you see the one-liner |
| Lines of code | ~8 | ~8 (plus a 6-line helper) |
| Extra memory | None | One `Map<String,Id>` ≤ 3 K entries (~50 KB) |

The map approach is the idiomatic Salesforce (and general Apex) pattern for "look up the first record in a list by a field value." It eliminates the inner loop entirely, makes the hot path a single `Map.get`, and costs a trivial amount of heap. There's no governor-limit concern because it's all in-memory with no SOQL or DML. I'd only prefer the nested loop if the list were expected to be tiny (say < 100 items) and the caller really wanted zero extra allocations, which isn't the case here.

