## Explanation of design decisions

| Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|
| **State-change detection via `Trigger.oldMap`** | We compare `oldCase.IsClosed` (false) with `c.IsClosed` (true) so the job fires only on the open → closed transition, not on every subsequent update to an already-closed case. |
| **Contact filter in the trigger** | The requirement says "Cases without a contact get no task." Filtering in the trigger avoids enqueueing a job that would immediately no-op. |
| **One `System.enqueueJob` per case** | The task explicitly asks for per-case isolation. If one case's insert fails (e.g., a validation rule on Task), the remaining jobs still execute. This also keeps each job's governor-limit footprint minimal (1 SOQL, 1 DML). |
| **Store only the `Id` in the constructor** | Queueable jobs are serialised into the async queue. Passing the full sObject can cause issues if the record changes before execution; storing the Id and re-querying in `execute` is the recommended pattern and guarantees we work with the latest data. |
| **Defensive null check in `execute`** | Even though the trigger filters on `ContactId != null`, the contact could theoretically be removed before the job runs (a few seconds to minutes later). The null-check prevents a `WhoId`-insert failure. |
| **`Date.today().addDays(2)`** | Computed at execution time, not at enqueue time, so the date is always exactly two days from the day the job actually runs. |
| **No recursion guard / handler class** | The trigger only creates `Task` records, so it cannot re-fire the Case trigger. A separate handler class would add indirection without benefit for this two-file deliverable. If the survey step grows (vendor integration), the logic can be extracted into a handler or a `PlatformEvent`-driven process without changing the trigger signature. |

