public class AccountCountrySync {

    /**
     * After-update handler: when an Account's BillingCountry changes, propagate the
     * new value to MailingCountry on that Account's contacts whose MailingCountry is
     * blank or still matches the *old* BillingCountry.
     *
     * Bulk-safe: one SOQL (scoped to affected Accounts), one DML, no loops over SOQL.
     */
    public static void handle() {

        // ── 1. Identify Accounts whose BillingCountry actually changed ──────────
        Set<Id>    changedAccountIds = new Set<Id>();
        Map<Id, String> oldCountryById = new Map<Id, String>();
        Map<Id, String> newCountryById = new Map<Id, String>();

        for (Integer i = 0; i < Trigger.new.size(); i++) {
            Account newAcc = Trigger.new[i];
            Account oldAcc = Trigger.old[i];

            // Apex == does value comparison and is null-safe for Strings
            if (newAcc.BillingCountry != oldAcc.BillingCountry) {
                changedAccountIds.add(newAcc.Id);
                oldCountryById.put(newAcc.Id, oldAcc.BillingCountry);
                newCountryById.put(newAcc.Id, newAcc.BillingCountry);
            }
        }

        // Nothing to do if no Account's country changed
        if (changedAccountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // ── 2. Single bulk-safe SOQL: contacts for ONLY the affected Accounts ──
        //
        // We deliberately do NOT query "FROM Contact" without a filter.
        // With 2.3 M contacts that would exceed the 50 000-row SOQL governor.
        // Scoping to the ≤ 200 AccountIds in the batch keeps the result set
        // small (a few thousand rows at most) while still being one query.
        //
        Map<Id, List<Contact>> contactsByAccountId = new Map<Id, List<Contact>>();
        for (Contact c : [
            SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds
        ]) {
            if (!contactsByAccountId.containsKey(c.AccountId)) {
                contactsByAccountId.put(c.AccountId, new List<Contact>());
            }
            contactsByAccountId.get(c.AccountId).add(c);
        }

        // ── 3. Pick the contacts that need updating ─────────────────────────────
        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();

        for (Id accId : changedAccountIds) {
            String oldCountry  = oldCountryById.get(accId);
            String newCountry  = newCountryById.get(accId);
            List<Contact> accContacts = contactsByAccountId.get(accId);

            if (accContacts == null) {
                continue; // no contacts on this Account
            }

            for (Contact c : accContacts) {
                // Update only when MailingCountry is blank (null or '')
                // or still equals the account's *previous* BillingCountry.
                // Contacts with a different MailingCountry (they work abroad)
                // are left untouched.
                if (String.isBlank(c.MailingCountry) || c.MailingCountry == oldCountry) {
                    c.MailingCountry = newCountry;
                    toUpdate.add(c);
                }
            }
        }

        // ── 4. Single bulk DML ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update toUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

## Explanation of the one change I made to your approach

You asked me to query **all** contacts with `[SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact]`. With 2.3 million contacts that query will hit the 50 000-row SOQL governor limit and throw `System.LimitException: SOQL queries: 50001` on the very first MDM batch of 200 accounts. It would also waste ~2 MB of heap pulling records you never need.

The fix is a single `WHERE` clause:

```sql
SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry
FROM Contact
WHERE AccountId IN :changedAccountIds
```

It is still **one query, no queries in loops**. We simply scope it to the ≤ 200 account IDs whose `BillingCountry` actually changed. In the worst realistic case (200 accounts × 250 contacts each) that is 50 000 rows—right at the limit but within it for a normal org. If your data modelling ever produces accounts with >250 contacts you can split the `IN` clause into chunks of 500 account IDs, but that is almost certainly not needed for a headquarters move.

Everything else—single query at the top, in-memory filtering, one DML statement—stays exactly as you described.

